Subject: Answer charCodeAt And charAt From A Primitive Receiver Without Converting

What this does
  js_string_charCodeAt and js_string_charAt read the character directly when
  this_val is already a JS_TAG_STRING and the index is a JS_TAG_INT.

Why we need it
  `s.charCodeAt(i)` spends most of its work before the read:
  JS_ToStringCheckObject is an identity on a primitive string plus a
  js_dup/JS_FreeValue refcount round trip, and JS_ToInt32Sat is a tagged dispatch
  that does nothing for an int.

What it adds
  A rope, a String object, a number receiver, null or undefined, a fractional,
  string or object index and a missing argument all still take the generic path.
  js_string_at is deliberately not served, because it accepts a negative index as
  an offset from the end. A negative int index is safe here: the unsigned bounds
  test rejects it, and out of range is NaN for charCodeAt and "" for charAt,
  which is what the generic path returns.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -52095,6 +52095,14 @@
     JSValue val, ret;
     JSString *p;
     int idx, c;
+
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(this_val) == JS_TAG_STRING &&
+        JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(argv[0]) == JS_TAG_INT) {
+        p = JS_VALUE_GET_STRING(this_val);
+        if (likely((uint32_t)JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[0]) < p->len))
+            return js_int32(string_get(p, JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[0])));
+        return JS_NAN;
+    }
 
     val = JS_ToStringCheckObject(ctx, this_val);
     if (JS_IsException(val))
@@ -52121,6 +52129,15 @@
     JSString *p;
     int idx, c;
 
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(this_val) == JS_TAG_STRING &&
+        JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(argv[0]) == JS_TAG_INT) {
+        p = JS_VALUE_GET_STRING(this_val);
+        if (likely((uint32_t)JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[0]) < p->len))
+            return js_new_string_char(ctx,
+                       string_get(p, JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[0])));
+        return js_empty_string(ctx->rt);
+    }
+
     val = JS_ToStringCheckObject(ctx, this_val);
     if (JS_IsException(val))
         return val;
